From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 16:38:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02623/11362] [outer-loop] Config Layer B: local gemma-12B
 reads tr87 scene, proposes is_win rule but it does NOT ground

The Carnot propose-then-ground test for the config WIN-RULE (Layer B). A local
open-weight GGUF (gemma-4-12B-it on the AMD iGPU, offline-legal per Decentralization
Rule 1, zero quota) reads tr87's 64x64 ASCII scene + editable-region bbox and emits a
checkable is_win(grid) predicate. The verifier grounds it against reality (tr87's rule
is the known glyph-rewrite, solved earlier by a GameAdapter).

Honest result: the 12B PROPOSED a structured rule (identified an editable region and a
reference region, hypothesized a region-match) but it did NOT ground -- fires_on_win=False,
false_positive_rate=0.0 over 5 non-wins. It read the scene STRUCTURE but inferred the wrong
relation for tr87's complex rewrite rule.

Findings: (a) the local 12B can propose structured scene-derived rules but is not strong
enough to derive the correct complex rewrite-class rule; (b) tr87 is the hardest config
rule-class (glyph rewrite) -- a simpler config game, or stronger scaffolding (show transitions,
hand the rule-grid region explicitly), is the natural next test; (c) the propose-then-ground
verification machinery works -- it correctly REJECTED the wrong rule rather than over-accepting.

Adversarial-verify clean (0 flags).

